>
>The spoilers I've read have said not to enchant Magicbane above +2,

  That's a Nethack Myth.  

The spoiler: <http://www.spod-central.org/~psmith/nh/art2-343.txt>
actually says:

"What is the best enchantment for Magicbane?  If you simply want to cause
the most damage, then the table indicates that you should enchant it as
high as possible (safely to +7).  Fortunately, your odds of magic-
resistance, curse-resistance, and engraving do not depend upon the
enchantment.  However, if you want to maximize the best magical attacks
of this artifact, it should be enchanted to +2."



  Lets pull out the two plain statements of fact in that paragraph:

  "If you simply want to cause the most damage, then the table 
indicates that you should enchant it as high as possible (safely 
to +7)."  

  "Fortunately, your odds of magic-resistance, curse-resistance, and 
engraving do not depend upon the enchantment."


  Seems pretty clear to me.  


  (NB. Magicbane's curse resistance nullifies 95% of curse attacks.  
Plain magic resistance reduces a curse attack to cursing d3 items.)


  Note too that magic-resistance and curse-resistance depend on 
Magicbane being *wielded*.  There are ascension posts out there
where the player is simply carrying Magicbane (carefully enchanted
to +2 mind you) in inventory, where Magicbane gives no benefits.

  It seems obvious to me that leaving Magicbane at +2 enchantment
to "maximize its best magical attacks" is worse than useless if
it means you don't wield Magicbane.  And, of course, if you don't
wield Magicbane you're guaranteed not to get any of those "maximized
best magical attacks."


  So maximally enchanting Magicbane seems worth it just because of
the increased desirability of wielding a more powerful Magicbane,
because Magicbane's "best magical effects" are magic resistance
and curse resistance, which you have to wield it to get.


  The final sentence quoted from the spoiler consists almost purely 
of weasel words:

  "However, if you want to maximize the best magical attacks
of this artifact, it should be enchanted to +2."


  Maximize is market-speak.  What's being maximized, just how
much it's actually improved, and what it's costing you to
maximize it are what's important.  (Hence all the commercials
that tout "maximum!" whatever.)

  Best is almost identical to maximum, except it's further qualified
to "magical attacks."  The "magical attacks" of Magicbane aren't
very magical, nor or they really attacks.  If it's the best,
it's the best of a very bad lot. (A tin opener is the "best" way
to open a tin.  Does anyone carry a tin opener for very long?)

  And let's look at them: confusion, scaring, probing and cancellation.
Engrave Elbereth (which Magicbane gives you in one turn whatever
its enchantment) gives you the best of those on every monster
around you 100% of the time, instead of rarely on one monster.
And the monster may resist.  And a confused, scared, probed and
canceled mumak will still kill you, unless you're standing on
Elbereth.

  Reminding the reader that Magicbane is an artifact is pointless.
Sting is an artifact, too.  That doesn't mean it should be used
for very long.


  The best magical effect would be cancellation.  And at best
the chance of cancellation is 10%.  Let's face it, if you got
10% on a test you failed very badly.

  And most monsters have nothing to cancel:

<http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/msg/4abfee5843f98c41?dmode=source&hl=en>


  186 out of 326 monsters have nothing to cancel.  A further
50 or so Magicbane's melee range, extremely low probability,
resistable cancel attack is useless against.

  That's not counting monsters with high magic resistance,
who can resist all the attacks.

  Now, are you planning on memorizing which monsters have something
worth canceling with a low probability at melee range and are
unlikely to resist?  Are you planning to further memorize what
can be canceled, and how to take advantage of it?  (How to make
use of cancellation depends on what's canceled.)

>because
>the higher its enchantment, the less often its special attacks
>(scare/stun/cancel) go off. 

  If you want a monster stunned/scared/confused, engraving Elbereth
with Magicbane gives a more useful form of those effects *100%* of
the time on *all* monsters around you *when* and *where* you want.
(I say more useful because a stunned/confused/scared monster can 
still kill you, which is much less likely if you are standing on 
Elbereth.)

  (Search the source code to see exactly what the effects of
scare/stun/confusion actually *are* on a monster.  I think you'll
be surprised, but not pleasantly.)

  Each extra point of enchantment gives another point of damage 
*per hit.*  At +7 that's four more points of damage per hit.

  How many times do you think you hit with Magicbane?  100 times?
That would be 400 more points of damage.  1000 times?  That would
be 4000 more points of damage.

  (Let's be fair and point out that you won't hit as often because
you'll be killing monsters faster.  And some of those extra points
of damage will be lost as overkill as well.  Those are good things, 
right?  Certainly no on complains about them when enchanting
Grayswandir.)

  If that looks good it's because it *is* good.  Everyone wants
to do more damage.  Why do you think Grayswandir gets recommended
so often?

  Or look at it like this: If you had a non-cursed +4 ring of 
increase damage, would you wear it?  Now, what if it didn't
have a hunger cost and didn't take up a ring slot?  Would you 
wear it then?

  That's what enchanting Magicbane up to +7 instead of +2 gives
you, +4 increase damage for the cost of the enchant weapon scrolls.
(Yes, +7 - +2 = 4 points of damage, when talking about Magicbane.)

  And that damage will accumulate.  If you get massively lucky
and cancel a monster twice by hitting it with Magicbane, you
won't gain anything from the second cancellation, even assuming
you gained something useful from the first cancellation.

>Is that not the case, or is the higher enchantment worth more 
>than the special attacks?

  If you have to ask this question, you've pretty much answered
it for yourself, because that means the special attacks have 
not been noticeably beneficial to you.  I can almost guarantee 
the benefits of +4 increased damage per hit will be noticeable 
to you.  


  I had one game where enchant weapon scrolls didn't show 
up until late.  Going from +0 to +5 Magicbane was a great
improvement.  And at +5 Magicbane still had a 5% chance of 
attempting to cancel a monster.  (Yeah, that's a damn low
possibility.  So is 10%.)

  So try Magicbane at +5.  If you find yourself longing for
any of the attempted special effects, curse Magicbane and 
engrave three letters with it.  That will get Magicbane down 
to +2.  (Don't forget to uncurse it afterwards.)



  Before I researched it, the only thing I noticed about 
Magicbane's special attacks was negative.  It's a lot harder
to kill a confused/scared monster on an altar.  (I did, and 
do, carry a separate weapon just to kill sacrifices, even 
though it generally takes longer.)

  In fact, the confusion/scaring attacks are generally negative.
They force me to hunt down monsters I want to kill.  (If I didn't
want to kill them I wouldn't have hit them in the first place.) 
If I want to avoid a monster, engraving Elbereth with Magicbane 
gives me that effect when I want, 100% guaranteed, on every monster
around me.

  And with hundreds of thousands of turns of wielding Magicbane
at +2 (or less) I can count on one hand the number of times it's
successfully canceled a spell caster and increased my max mana 
by a few points.  



